observe-response-ingress-over-websocket

Lane: cross

Unit: track-model-responses-by-attempt

ChildPatch: observe-response-ingress-over-websocket

Form: GitDiff

Segment: schema

Purpose: Publish and retain attempt-scoped WebSocket response facts independently of model output delivery and native conversation history.

Requires: Unit:cross/retain-model-diagnostics-for-thirty-six-hours, FlatPatch:backend/probe-models-after-weekly-quota-reset

Provides: Record setup, dispatch, receipt and terminal facts for generation, compaction, memory, guardian and native ephemeral probes. Publish ordered modelResponse/updated snapshots, retain the latest 250 attempts in memory and serve modelResponse/list filters and cursors. Asynchronously persist and restore through the shared native StateRuntime logs pool, page older records, flush the ordered writer and drain every accepted write before that pool closes. Expire cached records, model membership and last-generation facts with the shared 36-hour retention. Expose last-generation TPS and TTFC plus rolling 24-hour per-model TPS P50/P05, TTFC P50/P95 and separate valid sample counts. Supply per-model and aggregate recent-24H generation counts with successful Completed attempts as numerator and all attempts, including unfinished ones, as denominator. Retry transient SQLite busy writes in place with bounded delays while preserving publication order, clear the current persistence error after any successful write, and retain the latest error occurrence with its actual timestamp for transient presentation.

Boundary: HTTP, SSE and WebSocket warmup requests create no response-attempt records. Native transport, prewarming, fallback, incomplete-response errors and cancellation draining remain unchanged. Preserve reported incomplete usage and its original terminal outcome. Missing usage, including omitted cache_write_tokens, stays unknown, reported zero stays zero, observers never await storage, restored clocks remain inactive and current storage errors remain visible. Preserve nonincremental-reason and item-type diagnostics without tool counters or legacy readers. Typed records, native logs database migration and physical expiry belong to retain-model-diagnostics-for-thirty-six-hours. Atomic output, pricing, full-history accounting, probe scheduling and TUI presentation remain separate. Restore compact generation samples once and maintain timing distributions and outcome counts in memory without database reads on refresh. Timing distributions and latest-generation metrics use ended attempts only. Do not open an independent SQLite file or connection pool; explicit unavailable native storage preserves live observations and reports persistence errors. Native runtime shutdown owns the shared pool closure. Exhausted retries and non-lock write failures remain current until a successful write; their latest occurrence remains available after recovery without blocking paging, flush or shutdown.

Verification: Observation and progress tests preserve receipt facts, failures, deadlines and unknown usage. History and backup tests cover publication ordering, live ownership, bounded recent retention, configured database placement, restart, 36-hour expiry and rolling summaries beyond recent-record eviction. Outcome tests cover ongoing-to-terminal transitions, successful and unsuccessful endings, duplicate or stale restore, per-model and total counts and 24-hour expiry without altering timing samples. Native WebSocket, guardian and probe tests preserve attribution, incomplete usage, cancellation draining and probe persistence while excluding warmup and HTTP; App Server tests cover listing, notifications and grouped summaries. Shutdown tests cover accepted queued writes, shared-pool closure and reopening the standalone logs main file. Concurrent-writer tests cover delayed recovery, error clearing after both a later revision and an unrelated successful write, retained occurrence timestamps, disk paging, ordered final revisions and bounded exhaustion. Parsing and persistence tests distinguish omitted cache-write usage from an explicit zero through serialized records and SQL NULL.

